parts.body:
An [[accelerated-death-benefit]] pays part or all of the death benefit (or a special surrender value) to the owner while the insured is still alive. New York defines the qualifying conditions in Insurance Law § 1113(a)(1):

- terminal illness with a life expectancy of 12 months or less;
- a medical condition requiring extraordinary medical care or treatment, regardless of life expectancy;
- certain chronic illness conditions certified by a licensed health care practitioner (tied to federal tax definitions);
- residence in a nursing home for three months or more, expected to continue until death;
- end-of-life or palliative care for three months or more, expected to continue until death.
